---
/*
 * /longitudinal-patient-tracking/ — the problem-first page.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS
 * Outbound led with features ("a problem-oriented scribe", "problem-oriented fax triage")
 * and the most common reply was "my EHR already does that." At the feature level that is
 * true: every EHR has a problem list and a fax inbox. So this page never compares Stream
 * to the EHR. It compares Stream to the system a good clinician keeps by hand in their
 * notes — which works, and doesn't scale, doesn't transfer, and takes upkeep. Positioning
 * source: gtm/problem-first-messaging.md.
 *
 * Search: the terms are the page's second voice, not its first. Search Console (365d to
 * 2026-11-09) shows "ai in longitudinal patient management" at position 12.5 with no page
 * written for it, plus "problem-based charting" and "chart prep". The title and FAQ carry
 * those words; the headline carries the problem.
 *
 * CLAIM RULES: we ride alongside the EHR — no integration, no write-back, no replacement.
 * Every product claim here is already made on a product page (listed in the messaging
 * doc). Plan item tracking is early access and is labelled as such. No time-saved figures.
 */
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import CtaDark from '../../components/CtaDark.astro';
import { pricing } from '../../config/pricing';

const jamaUrl = 'https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2796664';

// One array feeds both the rendered FAQ and the FAQPage schema, so they cannot drift.
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is longitudinal patient tracking?',
    a: 'Following each of a patient’s problems across visits and sources: how it has changed, what has been tried and why it stopped, what the current plan is, and what still has to happen. In most charts that history sits in notes filed by date, so it is reconstructed by reading them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Doesn’t my EHR already have a problem list?',
    a: 'Yes, and it is useful as a list. What it usually does not hold is the history behind each entry — every plan, why it changed, what is outstanding — without opening notes in date order. Stream files each visit’s Assessment & Plan under its problem so that history is one view. Some EHRs do more of this than others, so the fairest test is the questions on this page, asked of your own workflow.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is problem-based charting?',
    a: 'Organizing documentation by medical problem rather than by visit date: each visit’s assessment and plan is written per problem, so the chart can be read one condition at a time. It is the idea behind Lawrence Weed’s problem-oriented medical record. Stream applies it to the note it drafts, so the structure does not depend on anyone maintaining it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Stream integrate with or replace my EHR?',
    a: 'Neither. Stream works alongside any EHR. The finished note pastes in with one click and your EHR remains the record of care. There is no write-back, which means the note lives in two places — the trade for working with whatever system you have, without an integration project.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can another clinician in my practice pick up my patients from it?',
    a: 'Yes. Clinicians in the practice open the same problem view, with each entry showing who wrote it. A covering clinician or a new partner reads the plan for a problem where it is filed, rather than decoding another clinician’s notes.',
  },
];
---
<Page readable="pillar"
  title="Longitudinal Patient Tracking for Primary Care, Alongside Your EHR | Stream"
  description="Keep every patient's problems moving between visits without maintaining a summary by hand. Problem-based tracking that works alongside any EHR — what it does, what it doesn't, and how it compares to keeping it in your notes.">

<Fragment slot="head">
  {/* Declares exactly the questions rendered below. */}
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  })} />
</Fragment>

<style>
