<section class="lpt">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="lpt-wrap">

      <span class="section-label">Longitudinal care</span>
      <h1>Keep the project, <em>not just the record.</em></h1>
      <p class="lpt-lede">Every patient you carry for years is a project: a set of problems, each with a history, a current plan and things that still have to happen. Your EHR stores the notes. Keeping the project current has always been left to you.</p>
      <p class="lpt-meta">Written for primary care and independent practices · Physician-authored</p>

      <h2>You probably already have a system</h2>
      <p>Most good clinicians do. A running problem list at the top of the note, carried forward and edited. Smart phrases. A private shorthand for <em>tried this, stopped it, here’s why</em>. For the patients you know well, it works.</p>
      <p>It has three costs, and they grow with your panel.</p>
      <div class="lpt-costs">
        <div class="lpt-cost">
          <h3>It doesn’t scale</h3>
          <p>It holds for the patients you remember. The one you haven’t seen in eight months is exactly the one whose summary is stale.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lpt-cost">
          <h3>It doesn’t transfer</h3>
          <p>It lives in your shorthand. A covering clinician or a new partner starts from your notes, not your system.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lpt-cost">
          <h3>It takes upkeep</h3>
          <p>The summary is re-typed and carried forward every visit, and it’s the first thing to slip on a hard week.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Copy-forward and templates are how clinicians keep a patient’s story in front of them, because notes give it nowhere else to live: nothing written in one stays pinned, and nothing is quick to review. It’s a sensible adaptation, and it is a large part of why <a href={jamaUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">half the text in the average chart is duplicated</a>.</p>

      <h2>What Stream does instead</h2>
      <p>Stream builds the same system as a side effect of documenting. Each visit’s note carries an Assessment &amp; Plan for every problem, and each one is filed under its problem. There is no summary to maintain, because the structure is the summary.</p>
      <ul class="lpt-steps">
        <li><span>Before the visit</span><div><a href="/features/huddle/">Huddle</a> shows active problems, problems that have fallen off your attention for ten months or more, and compliance flags.</div></li>
        <li><span>During it</span><div>Record ambiently or dictate. Anything you jot in the Scratchpad lands in the right section of the note.</div></li>
        <li><span>Across visits</span><div>Open a problem and read every plan written for it, in order — your chart, not AI output. Recap adds an AI-generated summary when you want one.</div></li>
        <li><span>After it</span><div>Follow-up tasks come out of the signed note, so the plan doesn’t depend on anyone remembering it. <a href="/features/task-management/">Task management</a></div></li>
        <li><span>From outside</span><div>Faxes and uploads are filed as excerpts under the problems they’re about, each linked to the page it came from. <a href="/intake/">Outside records</a></div></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The handoff is where it shows</h2>
      <p>The covering clinician, the new partner, the clinician who sees your patient between your visits: they open the same problem view you do. The plan for the CKD isn’t in your head or your shorthand. It’s filed under CKD, in order, with who wrote each entry.</p>

      <h2>Five questions to ask of your own workflow</h2>
      <p>Not of your EHR — of how you work today.</p>
      <table class="lpt-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>What it takes today</th><th>With Stream</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>When did you last change her levothyroxine, and why?</td><td>Open notes until you find it, or trust your summary.</td><td>Open the problem: every plan for it, in order.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Which of today’s patients has a problem nobody has touched in a year?</td><td>Re-read each chart before clinic.</td><td>Huddle’s falling-off view, before the visit.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The A1c in Tuesday’s cardiology fax: is it in her diabetes plan?</td><td>Someone reads it and retypes what matters.</td><td>An excerpt under diabetes, linked to its page.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>You’re covering for a partner. What was the plan for this patient’s CKD?</td><td>Decode your partner’s notes.</td><td>The same problem view your partner sees.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The follow-ups you planned last month: which happened?</td><td>Memory, or a list you keep yourself.</td><td>Tasks generated from the signed note.*</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="lpt-footnote">* Tracking each plan item through to completion is in <a href="/referral-tracking/">early access</a>.</p>

      <div class="lpt-honest">
        <h2>What Stream is not</h2>
        <p>It isn’t an EHR, and it doesn’t replace one. It doesn’t integrate or write back: the finished note pastes into any EHR in one click, and your EHR remains the record of care.</p>
        <p>That is a real cost — the note lives in two places — and it’s the trade for working alongside whatever system you already have, with no integration project. If write-back is a requirement, Stream is the wrong choice today.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <div class="lpt-faq">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

